1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,560
Good morning everyone.

2
00:00:01,560 --> 00:00:06,426
Last week we were looking at the hunter-gatherers in Ireland, across the Irish Sea

3
00:00:06,426 --> 00:00:07,150
from England.

4
00:00:07,150 --> 00:00:11,340
Today, we're going to move on to the period between four

5
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:15,450
and six thousand years ago, known as the Neolithic period,

6
00:00:15,450 --> 00:00:19,520
which is when a total farming economy was introduced in Ireland.

7
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:25,660
Now, there are several hypotheses about the origins of the first Neolithic settlers in

8
00:00:25,660 --> 00:00:26,062
Ireland,

9
00:00:26,062 --> 00:00:28,570
but most of these contain problems.

10
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:34,858
For instance, there are considerable archaeological difficulties about the theory that they came from

11
00:00:34,858 --> 00:00:35,340
England.

12
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,930
The evidence doesn't really add up.

13
00:00:37,930 --> 00:00:43,374
But there are even greater practical problems about the theory that they came directly

14
00:00:43,374 --> 00:00:44,680
from continental Europe.

15
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:49,639
For one thing, it's not clear just how sufficient numbers of men

16
00:00:49,639 --> 00:00:55,200
and women could have been transported to Ireland to establish a viable population.

17
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:02,628
As you know, the hunter-gatherer economy which existed beforehand was based on small scattered

18
00:01:02,628 --> 00:01:03,150
groups.

19
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:10,270
The farming economy would almost certainly have required much larger communities to do all the work needed to plant

20
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:13,640
and tend sufficient crops to sustain them through the year.

21
00:01:13,640 --> 00:01:19,330
The early farmers kept various animals, including cattle and sheep.

22
00:01:19,330 --> 00:01:21,744
There's also evidence of pigs,

23
00:01:21,744 --> 00:01:25,887
but it is possible that these could have been descended from the native wild

24
00:01:25,887 --> 00:01:26,430
species.

25
00:01:26,430 --> 00:01:31,985
Now, we know from modern farming that if the level of breeding stock falls

26
00:01:31,985 --> 00:01:37,820
below about three hundred females, the future of the species locally is at risk.

27
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:42,976
So we must assume that from the beginnings of Neolithic farming the number of

28
00:01:42,976 --> 00:01:46,267
breeding sheep would have considerably exceeded three hundred,

29
00:01:46,267 --> 00:01:50,360
and the national cattle herd must have been of a similar size.

30
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:54,021
The question is how these were brought to the area

31
00:01:54,021 --> 00:01:55,530
and where they came from.

32
00:01:55,530 --> 00:02:02,350
It's usually suggested that the Neolithic settlers used skin-covered boats to transport livestock.

33
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:07,290
But this method would have severely restricted the range of the colonizing fleets.

34
00:02:07,290 --> 00:02:13,980
The sheer volume of animal transport necessary means it's unlikely that this livestock could

35
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:16,270
have been brought from anywhere further than England.

36
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:19,381
What about crops?

37
00:02:19,381 --> 00:02:24,015
Well, two main cereal crops were introduced to Ireland during this time:

38
00:02:24,015 --> 00:02:27,630
wheat and barley, both in several varieties.

39
00:02:27,630 --> 00:02:33,370
The main evidence for their presence consists of impressions on pottery, where a cereal

40
00:02:33,370 --> 00:02:39,110
grain accidentally became embedded in the surface of a pot before it was fired.

41
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,940
The grain itself was destroyed by the firing,

42
00:02:41,940 --> 00:02:46,185
but it left an impression on the pot which could be studied

43
00:02:46,185 --> 00:02:47,600
and identified by botanists.

44
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:52,200
Let's turn our attention now to the farming technology available at that time.

45
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:57,584
Before the cereal crops could be planted, it would have been necessary to clear the forest

46
00:02:57,584 --> 00:02:59,940
and to break the ground by ploughing.

47
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:05,647
The stone blade of a plough has been discovered during excavation in County Mayo

48
00:03:05,647 --> 00:03:06,870
in western Ireland.

49
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:10,058
The body of the plough would have been of wood

50
00:03:10,058 --> 00:03:12,290
and could have been drawn by people,

51
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:14,840
but it's also likely that cattle were used.

52
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:20,860
Now, the cultivation of crops and the husbandry of livestock brought about changes in

53
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:25,160
people's lifestyle such as the type of shelters they made.

54
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:31,400
For one thing, instead of moving from place to place they needed permanent dwellings.

55
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:36,787
The stone axes used to chop down trees to make these dwellings were far

56
00:03:36,787 --> 00:03:40,250
superior to any that the Stone Age hunter-gatherers used.

57
00:03:40,250 --> 00:03:45,986
To make the axes, sources of suitable stone had to be found

58
00:03:45,986 --> 00:03:47,420
and systematically exploited.

59
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:53,525
These so-called 'axe factories' were really quarries rather than factories, as the manufacture of

60
00:03:53,525 --> 00:03:57,450
the axes wasn't regularly performed on the quarry site.

61
00:03:57,450 --> 00:04:02,230
However, after the axe had been chipped into shape, they needed water

62
00:04:02,230 --> 00:04:04,620
and sand for grinding and polishing,

63
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:09,400
so a high mountainside wouldn't have been an appropriate place for this.

64
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:14,996
So this final stage of the manufacture must have been carried out close to water

65
00:04:14,996 --> 00:04:19,100
and sure enough, there's ample evidence of this at coastal sites.

66
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:25,908
Now it's clear that these Neolithic axes were transported all over Ireland, as well as to Scotland

67
00:04:25,908 --> 00:04:27,910
and the south of England.

68
00:04:27,910 --> 00:04:33,452
It's not really surprising that axes from 'axe factories' in England have also been

69
00:04:33,452 --> 00:04:34,640
found in Ireland.

70
00:04:34,640 --> 00:04:39,190
At the very least, this indicates that there was a link between the two

71
00:04:39,190 --> 00:04:40,490
islands during that period.

72
00:04:40,490 --> 00:04:45,562
One of the most useful innovations of the colonizers was pottery making,

73
00:04:45,562 --> 00:04:48,520
which was quite unknown to Irish hunter-gatherers.

74
00:04:48,520 --> 00:04:52,978
The pottery was probably made by shaping clay into a ball with the hand,

75
00:04:52,978 --> 00:04:56,480
and then hollowing it until the walls were the right thickness.

76
00:04:56,480 --> 00:05:00,090
After firing, the outside was often polished.

77
00:05:00,090 --> 00:05:04,050
This would have helped the pots to retain water, as they weren't glazed.

78
00:05:04,050 --> 00:05:08,766
Now we know that the clay used usually came from local sources,

79
00:05:08,766 --> 00:05:14,269
which suggests that manufacture was on a fairly small scale, even though thousands of

80
00:05:14,269 --> 00:05:17,020
fragments are usually found at Neolithic sites.

81
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:21,120
In the course of time decoration began to appear.

82
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:25,227
At first this looked like a series of stitches and was just around the

83
00:05:25,227 --> 00:05:26,400
tops of the pots.

84
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:30,847
This could have been an imitation of earlier vessels which were made of leather

85
00:05:30,847 --> 00:05:31,800
sewn onto wood.

86
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:34,400
Then eventually pots with decoration all over...
